.lua
book.chapter(27, "Abstract syntax trees and visitors")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Generic syntax is an explicit data model"
.pp
ASTBuilder exposes Ast with Leaf tag text and Node tag children. Tags identify
syntactic roles; text carries leaf payloads; children preserve structural
order. This is a generic tree for languages you build, not a mutable view of
the interpreter's checked MetaML expressions. A node tagged add receives
meaning only from your parser, validator, and interpreter. It does not acquire
integer typing automatically because MetaML itself supports addition.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[tree := Node "add" [Leaf "num" "1", Leaf "num" "2"]
Test.equal "node count" (ASTBuilder.size tree) 3
Test.equal "inspection" (ASTBuilder.dump tree) "(add (num 1) (num 2))"
Test.equal "child tags" (List.map ASTBuilder.tag (ASTBuilder.children tree)) ["num", "num"]
Test.equal "leaf payload" (ASTBuilder.text (Leaf "num" "7")) (Some "7")]=],
    "Construct and inspect a generic tree")
.endlua
.pp
ASTBuilder supplies size, depth, tag, text, children, searches, rebuilding,
parsing, and S-expression conversion. Handle conversion errors explicitly.
Generic syntax can contain unexpected tags or arities even when the enclosing
MetaML program type-checks. Establish a validation boundary before semantic
passes assume that every addition has exactly two children or every numeric
leaf contains a valid integer.
.sh 2 "Traversal order is an algorithmic choice"
.pp
Preorder visits a parent before its descendants, postorder visits children
before their parent, and breadth-first traversal visits by increasing depth.
Preorder suits inherited context such as entering a scope. Postorder suits
synthesized results such as evaluating operands before an operator. Breadth
first is useful for level-oriented inspection. Choose the order from the
information a callback needs rather than from whichever function name is
shortest.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[tree := Node "add" [Leaf "num" "1", Node "neg" [Leaf "num" "2"]]
Test.equal "preorder" (List.map ASTBuilder.tag (WalkAST.preorder tree))
  ["add", "num", "neg", "num"]
Test.equal "postorder" (List.map ASTBuilder.tag (WalkAST.postorder tree))
  ["num", "num", "neg", "add"]
Test.equal "breadth first" (List.map ASTBuilder.tag (WalkAST.breadth_first tree))
  ["add", "num", "neg", "num"]]=], "Order determines when information is available")
.endlua
.pp
WalkVisitor adds effect-oriented traversal, depth callbacks, enter/leave hooks,
and child-index paths. A path is a list of zero-based indices from the root.
At_path returns Option for checked navigation. Such paths are locations in
one tree shape, not permanent identities: inserting or removing a child can
invalidate saved paths. Use stable domain identifiers when an editor or
analysis must retain identity across shape-changing rewrites.
.sh 2 "A fold replaces syntax with meaning"
.pp
VisitorBuilder.make constructs a folding visitor from a leaf handler and a
node handler. The node handler receives already-computed child results.
This abstracts the recursion while leaving semantic interpretation in user
code. A counting visitor, an evaluator, and a renderer can share traversal
structure but produce different result types. The fold is especially useful
when every node's result depends only on its own tag and child results.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[tree := Node "sum" [Leaf "num" "1", Leaf "num" "2"]
evaluate := VisitorBuilder.make
  (fn _ text -> Int.from_string text |> Option.with_default 0)
  (fn _ children -> List.sum children)
Test.equal "folded result" (evaluate tree) 3
Test.equal "tag counts" (VisitorBuilder.count_tags tree) [("num", 2), ("sum", 1)]
Test.equal "path lookup" (WalkVisitor.at_path [1] tree) (Some (Leaf "num" "2"))]=],
    "A simple fold over a deliberately restricted tree vocabulary")
.endlua
.pp
The example defaults malformed leaf text to zero solely because its tiny
input vocabulary is controlled. A real compiler should return Result from
validation or interpretation rather than silently assign semantics to bad
syntax. Likewise, ignoring every node tag is appropriate for a generic count
but not for an arithmetic interpreter. A reusable traversal does not absolve
the semantic handler from checking its domain.
.sh 2 "Rewrite direction controls available information"
.pp
Bottom-up mapping transforms children before presenting the rebuilt parent
to the callback. Constant folding naturally follows this order: children
that were expressions may now be literals. Top-down mapping transforms a
node before visiting the resulting descendants, useful when a parent rewrite
establishes context or changes the structure to inspect. Document whether
newly introduced nodes are traversed and whether another pass is required
to reach the desired normal form.
.lua
book.algorithm("Bottom-up constant folding", {
    "Recursively transform each child in source order.",
    "Rebuild the current node from those transformed children.",
    "If the node is a recognized operator with literal operands:",
    {1, "Compute using the source language's arithmetic contract."},
    {1, "Replace the operator with a literal carrying the result."},
    "Otherwise preserve the rebuilt node."
})
.endlua
.pp
WalkAST.rewrite uses an optional replacement: None means retain the node,
not delete it. Deletion changes a parent's child collection and needs a
separate representation of that intent. Distinguish one traversal from
repeated normalization; applying a rewrite once does not prove no rule can
match the result. If repeatedly applying rules, establish termination or
use an explicit step budget and report whether the budget was exhausted.
.sh 2 "Preserve the information your clients need"
.pp
A generic tree's text and tag fields do not automatically retain comments,
source spans, inferred types, or binding identities. Decide which annotations
belong in your domain representation and how transformations preserve them.
A renderer that only needs semantic structure can discard trivia; an editor
cannot. An optimizer may regard source locations as irrelevant to equality
while still needing them to explain an error after transformation.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Treat each pass as a contract over trees. State its accepted vocabulary,
preserved annotations, output invariants, and failure representation before
choosing whether to implement it with a fold, a walk, or a rewrite callback.
.pp
Read ASTBuilder, WalkAST, WalkVisitor, and VisitorBuilder in metalib/cpp and
their D mirrors. Construct a malformed binary operator and write a validator
that rejects its arity before evaluation. Then implement bottom-up folding
and test that interpretation is preserved for nested expressions. Include
a tree whose saved child path becomes stale after a rewrite, and explain
why structural location and semantic identity require different mechanisms.
